# Apache Ranger — PR authoring rules

Goal: land a PR in `apache/ranger` with **zero avoidable review rounds**.

Three gates decide whether a PR merges:

1. **The build.** Checkstyle, sortpom and the enforcer plugin fail the build on violation. A red
   build is not reviewed. → §2, §3
2. **Correctness in a concurrent, long-lived process.** Ranger's policy engine is built once and
   read by every request thread. A large share of Ranger's historical bug fixes are the *same
   handful of mistakes* made in new code. → §4
3. **Committer review.** Ranger's most frequent reviewer is **Madhan Neethiraj (`mneethiraj`)** —
   ~900 authored commits and reviewer on most PRs. His feedback is highly consistent and almost
   entirely predictable. → §5

Gate 1 is mechanical. Gates 2 and 3 are what cost weeks. Read §4 and §5 *before* writing code.

Sources for this file: `dev-support/checkstyle.xml`, the root `pom.xml`, `.github/workflows/ci.yml`,
review comments on ~105 sampled PRs, and the commit history of `apache/ranger` (JIRA ids cited
below are real commits — `git log --grep RANGER-xxxx` to read them).

---

## 1. Pre-flight checklist

- [ ] An ASF JIRA exists: <https://issues.apache.org/jira/browse/RANGER>
- [ ] PR title starts with the JIRA id: `RANGER-XXXX: <short description>`
- [ ] **The commit message** also starts with the same JIRA id, lowercase after the colon
      (house style: `RANGER-5442: replace repeated Gson instantiation with JsonUtils methods`)
- [ ] PR body uses `.github/pull_request_template.md` — both sections filled in
- [ ] `mvn -T 8 clean verify` passes locally (exactly what CI runs)
- [ ] No binary artifacts, tarballs, jars or IDE files in the diff
- [ ] Diff contains **only** what the JIRA describes — no drive-by reformatting
- [ ] §4 self-check done: no new shared mutable state, no lazy init, no `!= null` on config strings
- [ ] Every new/changed config property follows §6.3 naming
- [ ] If DB schema changed: **all five** flavors updated (§6.6)
- [ ] If a public/plugin-facing API changed: backward compatibility considered and stated (§5.1)

---

## 2. The build is the first reviewer

CI (`.github/workflows/ci.yml`) runs on JDK 17:

```bash
mvn -T 8 clean verify --no-transfer-progress -B -V
```

Run the same thing locally. For a fast loop on one module:

```bash
mvn -pl <module> -am -DskipTests verify
```

`verify` — not `compile`, not `install -DskipTests` — is the phase that runs Checkstyle. A build
that only "compiles" proves nothing.

### What fails the build

| Plugin | Phase | Fails on |
|---|---|---|
| `maven-checkstyle-plugin` (checkstyle 8.29) | `verify` | any violation of `dev-support/checkstyle.xml` — **including test sources** (`includeTestSourceDirectory=true`, `checkstyle.failOnViolation=true`) |
| `sortpom-maven-plugin` | `validate` | any `pom.xml` not in sorted order (`verifyFail=Stop`, `verifyFailOn=Strict`) |
| `maven-enforcer-plugin` | `validate` | JDK < 17, Maven < 3.6.3, or a banned dependency |

If you touch any `pom.xml`, run `mvn sortpom:sort` before committing — hand-editing a pom almost
always breaks the sort check.

### Banned dependencies (enforcer, `searchTransitive=true`)

- `junit:junit` / `junit:junit-dep` at `compile`/`runtime`/`provided` — the project is on **JUnit 5**
  (`org.junit.jupiter`).
- `com.sun.jersey:*` / `com.sun.jersey.contribs:*` — the project is on **Jersey 2.x**
  (`org.glassfish.jersey.*`). Only `test`-scoped Jersey 1 is tolerated, for Hadoop mini-cluster
  test infrastructure.

`spotbugs.failOnViolation` is `false`, so SpotBugs warnings do not break the build — but they are
visible to reviewers, and PMD violations have needed their own follow-up commits
(`RANGER-4822`, `RANGER-4196`). Fix them before pushing.

