## 6. Ranger conventions

### 6.1 Preferred libraries (measured across the codebase)

| Use | Do not use |
|---|---|
| `org.apache.commons.lang3.StringUtils` | `org.apache.commons.lang.*` (commons-lang 2 — being removed, RANGER-5391) |
| `org.apache.commons.collections.CollectionUtils` / `MapUtils` (**v3**, ~330 imports) | `commons.collections4` (4 imports repo-wide — do not introduce more) |
| `org.apache.ranger.authorization.utils.JsonUtils` | `new Gson()`, `new ObjectMapper()` at a call site |
| `org.apache.ranger.authorization.utils.StringUtil` (Ranger helpers) | reimplementing them |
| `org.slf4j.Logger` / `LoggerFactory` | `org.apache.log4j.*` (removed in RANGER-5495) |
| `com.fasterxml.jackson.*` | `org.codehaus.jackson.*` |
| plain loops and `CollectionUtils` | Guava (8 imports repo-wide; `guava` dependency removed in RANGER-4845) |
| explicit null checks | `java.util.Optional` (1 import repo-wide; static-importing it is a Checkstyle error) |

Streams are used, but sparingly (~48 call sites across 258 files in `agents-common`) and not in the
per-request authorization hot path. In a hot path, prefer an explicit loop.

### 6.2 Java style (what the codebase actually looks like)

Reference files: `authz-api/.../model/RangerAuthzRequest.java` (new model class),
`agents-common/.../policyengine/RangerPolicyEngineImpl.java` (engine code),
`agents-common/.../model/RangerBaseModelObject.java` (collection helpers).

**Vertical alignment.** Consecutive field declarations, constants and assignments are aligned
(`ALIGN_GROUP_FIELD_DECLARATIONS`, `ALIGN_CONSECUTIVE_ASSIGNMENTS` in
`dev-support/RangerCodeScheme-IntelliJ.xml`). Unaligned code reads as foreign and gets reformatted:

```java
private final PolicyEngine                 policyEngine;
private final RangerAccessRequestProcessor requestProcessor;
private final ServiceConfig                serviceConfig;

public RangerPolicyEngineImpl(...) {
    policyEngine     = new PolicyEngine(servicePolicies, pluginContext, roles, isUseReadWriteLock);
    serviceConfig    = new ServiceConfig(servicePolicies.getServiceConfig());
    requestProcessor = new RangerDefaultRequestProcessor(policyEngine);
}
```

Align only within a consecutive block; don't pad unrelated lines.

**Member order.** `private static final Logger LOG` first, then other `static final` constants
grouped **by visibility** (`public` block, then `private` block) and aligned within each group, then
instance fields, then constructors, then static factory methods, then public methods, then private
methods.

**Single `ret`, single return.** The dominant idiom is one result variable named `ret`, assigned in
branches and returned once (~560 `return ret;` sites in `agents-common` alone):

```java
public static <T> List<T> getUpdatableList(List<T> curr) {
    final List<T> ret;

    if (curr instanceof ArrayList) {
        ret = curr;
    } else {
        ret = curr != null ? new ArrayList<>(curr) : new ArrayList<>();
    }

    return ret;
}
```

Note `final List<T> ret;` **declared without an initializer** and definitely assigned in every
branch — that is the preferred form when the value is set exactly once (`ExplicitInitialization`
forbids `= null` anyway, and RANGER-4891 converted `ClientResponse response = null;` to
`final ClientResponse response;`). Use `X ret = <default>;` only when branches genuinely accumulate.

Short guard clauses do exist (notably in `equals()`), so this is a strong preference rather than an
absolute ban — but a method returning from five places will be asked to use `ret`.

**Generics.** Always the diamond operator: `new ArrayList<>()`, never `new ArrayList<String>()`
(swept in RANGER-5017).

**Braces.** `} else {`, `} else if (…) {`, `} catch (…) {`, `} finally {` stay on one line with the
closing brace. In `equals()`, chain with `else if` rather than sequential `if`s.

**Model classes.** Jackson annotations (`@JsonAutoDetect(fieldVisibility = ANY)`,
`@JsonInclude(NON_EMPTY)`, `@JsonIgnoreProperties(ignoreUnknown = true)`), aligned fields,
telescoping constructors delegating via `this(...)`, `Objects.hash` / `Objects.equals`. Setters use
the null-safe helpers, mutators use the updatable helpers:

```java
public void setPolicyItems(List<RangerPolicyItem> policyItems) {
    this.policyItems = nullSafeList(policyItems);
}

public boolean addPolicyItem(RangerPolicyItem policyItem) {
    policyItems = getUpdatableList(policyItems);
    ...
}
```

**Logging.** slf4j with `{}` placeholders — never string concatenation, and no
`if (LOG.isDebugEnabled())` around a parameterized call. The `==>` / `<==` entry/exit convention is
used at 400+ sites in `agents-common`; follow it in the class you are editing:

```java
LOG.debug("==> RangerAdminRESTClient.getRolesIfUpdated({}, {})", lastKnownRoleVersion, lastActivationTimeInMillis);
...
LOG.debug("<== RangerAdminRESTClient.getRolesIfUpdated({}, {}): {}", lastKnownRoleVersion, lastActivationTimeInMillis, ret);
```

Reviewers also ask for an `info` log where a change takes effect:

> "Add a info level log here: `LOG.info(\"Deleted {} pluginInfo records, for criteria={}\", ...)`" — PR #683

**Perf tracing.** In policy-engine paths, wrap with the standard guard so nothing is built when
tracing is off:

```java
RangerPerfTracer perf = null;

if (RangerPerfTracer.isPerfTraceEnabled(PERF_POLICYENGINE_REQUEST_LOG)) {
    perf = RangerPerfTracer.getPerfTracer(PERF_POLICYENGINE_REQUEST_LOG, "RangerPolicyEngine.evaluatePolicies(...)");
}
```

**Blank lines** are deliberate: one after a declaration block, around `if`/`try` blocks, and before
`return`. Dense unbroken method bodies get reformatted.

### 6.3 Configuration property naming

Plugin-side: `ranger.plugin.<serviceType>.<area>.<setting>`. Admin-side: `ranger.admin.<area>.<setting>`.

> "to be consistent with rest of policy related plugin configurations, I suggest using property
> prefix `ranger.plugin.<serviceType>.policy.` I suggest renaming `cache.refresh.mode` to
> `refresh.synchronous`, with default value as `false` —
> `ranger.plugin.hive.policy.refresh.synchronous`.
> `isSynchronousPolicyCacheUpdateEnabled()` => `isSynchronousPolicyRefresh()`" — PR #595

> "I suggest renaming the property to `ranger.admin.userstore.include.usergroups.mappings`." — PR #1185

**Rule:** name the property by *where it is read*, keep accessor and property names in sync, default
a new opt-in flag to `false`, and read it once into a `final` field at init (§4.2, §5.7).

### 6.4 REST API conventions

- `{id}` and `{name}` are separate endpoints, not overloads of one path:
  > "to be consistent with use of `{id}` and `{name}` in APIs, I would suggest to retain `{id}` in
  > this API and introduce another API for using `{name}` - like:
  > `/api/roles/name/{name}/addUserAndGroups`." — PR #135
- Prefer query params over a request body for a list of ids on `DELETE`:
  > "would marking `resourceIds` parameter as `@QueryParam(\"id\")` help to receive the IDs as query
  > params instead of body?" — PR #932
- Guard every endpoint with `@PreAuthorize("@rangerPreAuthSecurityHandler.isAPIAccessible(...)")`,
  consistent with sibling endpoints (§5.8).
- Never derive an identity used for authorization from a caller-supplied field in the view object
  (PR #788).

### 6.5 Tests

- **JUnit 5 only** (`org.junit.jupiter`); JUnit 4 is banned by the enforcer.
- `assert` is banned by Checkstyle — use `Assertions.assertEquals` etc.
- Test sources are checkstyle-checked; the same formatting rules apply.
- **No time-, timezone- or order-dependent tests.** Intermittent test failures have needed their own
  fix commits (RANGER-5153, RANGER-5461, RANGER-5480 `ConcurrentModificationException`,
  RANGER-5527). Don't rely on `HashMap`/`HashSet` iteration order, the default timezone, or
  wall-clock timing.
- For policy-evaluation changes, assert the *policy id* that produced the result, not just
  allow/deny — that is what catches an evaluator picking the wrong matching policy (RANGER-5429).
- Python: `intg/src/test/python/test_ranger_client.py`, one `TestCase` per client area.

### 6.6 Database changes

> "Database scripts for other flavors (example: MySQL, Oracle) should be updated as well." — PR #1120

A schema change means **all** of `security-admin/db/{mysql,postgres,oracle,sqlanywhere,sqlserver}`
plus the `PatchXxx_J100nn` Java patch class. Updating only Postgres is an automatic review round.
(`sqlserver` is being retired — check current master: *"`sqlserver` is no more supported."* — PR #1057.)

Also ask whether a patch is needed at all:

> "Are changes in `PatchForOzoneServiceDefPolicyConditionUpdate_J10065.java` necessary, given
> `RangerServiceDefService.java` handles service-def updates based on feature-flag ... If no
> additional cases are handled here, I suggest reverting" — PR #1073

Oracle is the flavor that breaks: RANGER-5002 "fix schema creation/migration failures with Oracle
database". Test against it if you can.

### 6.7 Docker / dev-support

- Container-specific scripts and configs go in container-specific directories (RANGER-4898).
- Ship one config file per flavour and copy the right one at container init, rather than rewriting
  XML programmatically at runtime (PR #920).
- Don't download large artifacts on every build (PR #302).
- Don't start services a compose file doesn't need (PR #1057).

